data.reasoningText:
Now I see the issue. The onChange handlers are directly accessing `api.target.value` without checking if `api.target` exists. The test is passing an empty object, which causes the error.

The fix is to make the onChange handlers defensive by checking if `api.target` exists before trying to access its `value` property. Here's the fix:

Instead of:
```typescript
onChange: (api: any) => {
  selectedProduct = (api.target as HTMLSelectElement).value;
},
```

We should do:
```typescript
onChange: (api: any) => {
  if (api?.target?.value) {
    selectedProduct = api.target.value;
  }
},
```

Or even simpler:
```typescript
onChange: (api: any) => {
  selectedProduct = (api?.target as HTMLSelectElement)?.value ?? selectedProduct;
},
```

Let me make these fixes to all three onChange handlers.

